    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Kemps is a relatively modern American card game that emerged in the 20th century. The exact origin is unclear, but it has been popular in the United States since at least the mid-1900s. The game is also known as Kent in some regions.',
      originCountries: [
        'USA',
        'France',
        'Switzerland',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Mid-20th century: Game emerges in USA',
        'Late 20th century: Spreads as popular party game',
        'Present day: Played at parties and gatherings',
        'Modern era: Available in app form',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game evolved from older four-of-a-kind collection games. The unique elements are the secret signals between partners and the simultaneous card exchange from the center layout, creating a game of observation and deception.',
      cultural: 'Kemps is popular as a party game and among groups of friends. The secret signal aspect makes it a social and often humorous game. It is commonly played at summer camps, college dorms, and casual gatherings.',
